{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfeX5TEhDGo4HAMZUhKTx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate, named `bayes_pattern_vs_alternating_coin`, and it passes the self-check (`OK`). The smoke-fit ELPD-LOO was -1203.5, against -1088.2 for the current best model (`personal_alternation_ideal`). That comparison means little yet: the smoke fit is short and single-chain, and its PSIS-LOO was flagged unreliable (2.8% of trials had Pareto k above 0.5, over the 1% tolerance).\n\n**Hypothesis:** this follows the brief's \"normative account plus one distortion\". People decide whether a sequence came from a random coin or from a noisy repeating pattern. A pattern is a template of period 1–4 (like HHHH, HTHT or HHTT) copied with occasional errors. The one distortion is that each person believes a fair coin switches sides at their own rate, often more than half the time. So alternation counts as evidence for randomness until the sequence becomes regular enough that a repeating template explains it better.\n\n**What it targets in the critique:** all three discrepancies that survived the FDR correction.\n- **Periodic patterns:** templates like HTHT and HHTT are directly evidence against randomness.\n- **Long runs:** a period-1 template (all heads or all tails) explains streaky sequences well, so they look less random.\n- **Perfect alternation:** a period-2 template explains HTHT... exactly, which stops the over-alternation belief from favouring it without limit.\n\n**How it is built:**\n- The pattern side is fixed: `compute_features` calculates each sequence's log marginal likelihood under the template generator, averaging over periods and templates and integrating out the copying-error rate analytically.\n- There are three free parameters: a population mean and spread for the believed switch rate (with 400 person slots, so the model can predict new participants), and a decision sensitivity `beta`.